+# RouteBudget Lab — Audit Correction Ledger
+
+**Date:** 2026-11-01  
+**Project:** RouteBudget Lab  
+**Subject:** Explicit verification and correction of previously reported narrative claims (Prompt Section 3 & 17)
+
+---
+
+## Overview
+
+In accordance with Section 3 of the Master Audit Prompt, all statements in previous narrative reports were treated as claims to verify against raw experimental files and C++ engine implementation. Below is the itemized correction ledger recording each claim, the verified empirical evidence, the corrected scientific statement, and the remedial action taken.
+
+---
+
+## Itemized Correction Ledger
+
+| # | Topic / Target Claim | Supporting Evidence / Inspection Finding | Corrected Scientific Statement | Action Taken |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| **1** | **“Guaranteed strict deadline compliance”** | The solver runs on macOS (a general-purpose, non-real-time operating system) without hard real-time kernel guarantees. Under `RESUMPTION_NO_PA`, 3 out of 32 runs overran by up to 2.52 ms. While reserving 2.0 ms (`TERMINAL_RESERVE_CONTROL`) yielded 0.0 $\mu$s observed overruns across all 192 runs, this is an empirical heuristic on tested instances, not a formal mathematical guarantee. | "Reserving 2.0 ms before the planning deadline empirically eliminated deadline overshoots across all 192 evaluated runs (0.0 $\mu$s observed overshoot vs 2,524.0 $\mu$s baseline max), but does not constitute a hard real-time operating system guarantee." | Qualified claim across all design, evaluation, and summary documents. |
+| **2** | **“Completely eliminating the historical 10.49% overhead”** | Telemetry audit of all 162,396 interruptions shows that while 98.83% (160,496) were skipped before dependency graph construction, full candidate extraction and kinematics validation still consumed 2.70 $\mu$s per interruption (0.438 s total CPU time across 32 runs). Furthermore, for the 1.17% (1,900) non-skipped interruptions, graph construction and selection still ran. | "The cheap optimistic filter ($U == 0$) bypasses expensive dependency graph building on 98.83% of interruptions in 14.3 ns, reducing per-interruption overhead by ~95.6% (from ~62 $\mu$s to 2.71 $\mu$s). However, calling extraction across all 162,396 interruptions still accumulated 0.44s of overhead, displacing ~61 iterations." | Refined overhead claims in `docs/selective_recovery_analysis.md` and added Section 11 timing breakdown. |
+| **3** | **“Definitively answers the research question”** | Scientific evaluations are inherently bounded by their parameter space (50–100 agents, 1.0s–5.0s budgets, $k=8$ neighborhood size, Moving AI grid maps). | "The empirical evidence definitively answers the research question within the evaluated parameter space (50–100 agents, 1.0s–5.0s budgets, $k=8$ neighborhood size, $K=2$ store capacity on Moving AI benchmark maps)." | Replaced absolute claims with appropriately bounded scientific statements. |
+| **4** | **“Kinematic safety”** | RouteBudget Lab operates on discrete spatio-temporal grid maps where agents move one grid cell per discrete timestep ($\Delta x + \Delta y \le 1$). Continuous vehicle kinematics, acceleration, turn radius, and momentum are not modeled. | "The system validates discrete spatio-temporal collision safety (start/goal matching, obstacle clearance, vertex collisions, edge swaps, and persistent stay-at-goal occupancy)." | Replaced "kinematic safety" with "discrete spatio-temporal collision safety" across documentation. |
+| **5** | **Exact nanosecond latency (14.3 ns)** | Apple Silicon M3 `std::chrono::steady_clock` uses `mach_absolute_time()` with a 24 MHz timebase (~41.67 ns tick resolution). A single measurement of a 5-instruction scalar loop cannot be reliably timed individually below clock resolution. The 14.3 ns figure is a batch average ($2,324.2\,\mu\text{s} / 162,396$ calls). | "The inner loop of `computeOptimisticUpperBound` averages 14.3 ns per call across 162,396 evaluations. Individual measurements are bounded by the 41.7 ns clock tick resolution and timer invocation overhead; end-to-end extraction and validation requires 2.70 $\mu$s." | Documented timing resolution, batch averaging methodology, and extraction overhead in analysis scripts and reports. |
+| **6** | **Broad claims that eviction opportunities never exist** | Eviction opportunities were not observed under $K=2$ FIFO capacity and 5.0 ms slice duration because resumed repairs drained suspended checkpoints before a 3rd repair caused an eviction. This does not prove that evictions never occur under smaller store capacities ($K=1$) or shorter slice durations. | "Under the evaluated configuration ($K=2$ FIFO capacity, 5.0 ms slice duration), natural store evictions were not observed because resumed repairs drained the store before capacity eviction occurred. In controlled unit tests with $K=1$, eviction recovery successfully executed." | Clarified operational scope and separated natural benchmark behavior from controlled unit tests. |
+| **7** | **Test coverage claims without explicit matrix** | The previous report cited 15 tests, but Section 12 of the prompt defined 22 specific required correctness and failure-path test conditions. | "All 22 test conditions specified in Section 12 of the Master Prompt are implemented as dedicated test functions in `tests/selective_recovery_tests.cpp` and pass 100% in both Release and ASan/UBSan builds." | Expanded `tests/selective_recovery_tests.cpp` from 15 to 22 test functions and generated [requirements_to_tests_matrix.md](file:///Users/disasgamage/Documents/GitHub/RouteBudgetLab/docs/requirements_to_tests_matrix.md). |
+| **8** | **Baseline commit described as current HEAD** | Baseline commit was previously cited as `b4d9f9e`, which was HEAD before subsequent selective recovery implementation. | "Historical baseline: `b4d9f9e`; Pre-audit recovery implementation: `07973d0`; Audited and completed state: current HEAD." | Delineated all three commit identities in preservation manifest and documentation. |
+| **9** | **Broken multiline sanitizer build commands** | Markdown documentation contained broken multiline shell commands that failed when pasted into standard shells. | Single-line and properly backslash-escaped build and test commands verified directly in the working terminal. | Corrected all build and test commands in README and technical report. |
+| **10** | **43,163 vs 43,166 snapshot count** | Grep of raw files and codebase revealed that `43163` was a truncated substring of an anytime cost timestamp (`0.431633s`). The true, verified count across all 24 raw JSON files is exactly **43,166** snapshots. | "The authoritative snapshot count across all 24 validation files in `results/partial_acceptance_offline/raw/` is exactly 43,166 distinct snapshots with $r_{\text{done}} \ge 1$. The figure 43,163 was an erroneous transcription." | Reconciled count directly from raw JSON files and documented exact derivation. |
+| **11** | **Selected negative candidates vs any negative candidates** | Raw JSON inspection revealed: Total candidates = 215,673; Negative candidates = 2,756 across 2,673 snapshots. Only **8 snapshots** contained a selected negative candidate in the optimal closure. | "Across 43,166 snapshots, 2,673 snapshots contained at least one negative candidate (2,756 negative paths total). Exactly 8 snapshots contained a negative candidate selected by the optimal closure to unlock larger positive gains (+155 SoC net gain)." | Explicitly separated snapshots containing any negative candidate from snapshots with selected negative candidates in optimal closures. |
