+## 5. Discussion & Scientific Decision
+
+### 5.1 Why Selective Recovery Does Not Outperform Checkpoint Resumption
+1. **Resumption Already Captures the Work:** When an interrupted repair carries an improving route prefix, macro-prefix checkpoint resumption preserves that prefix and completes the remaining neighborhood agents on the next slice. Once completed as a full repair, the entire improvement is committed without paying dependency closure overhead.
+2. **Natural Eviction Absence:** Under $K=2$ store capacity, the resumption manager prioritizes resuming suspended checkpoints on the immediately following slice. Because suspended checkpoints are resumed before a 3rd repair is initiated, checkpoints are almost never naturally evicted.
+3. **Terminal Sparsity:** At the terminal deadline, the 1–2 checkpoints suspended in the store almost invariably have $U == 0$ (95.7% of examined checkpoints had $U == 0$; none yielded feasible positive gain).
+4. **Displaced Search Penalty:** Evaluating partial acceptance on every slice interruption, even with the 98.83% filter skip rate, incurs 2.70 $\mu$s of extraction overhead per call. Across tens of thousands of interruptions, this accumulated latency displaced ~61 search iterations, causing 4 warehouse instances under tight 1.0s budgets to miss an improving full repair.
+
+### 5.2 Formal Decision
+In accordance with Stopping Rule B and Rule C (Prompt Section 16):
+> **Verdict: `OVERHEAD REDUCTION & SAFE TERMINAL DEADLINE COMPLIANCE ONLY; NOT BENEFICIAL AS A RECOVERY ACCELERATOR; RETAIN RESUMPTION AS DEFAULT`.**
+
+- The cheap optimistic gain filter ($U = \sum \max(w_i, 0)$) and the 2.0 ms terminal reserve are verified engineering contributions that successfully reduce overhead and enforce strict deadline compliance.
+- Selective recovery does not provide an additive solution quality improvement over checkpoint resumption alone. Ordinary checkpoint resumption (`RESUMPTION_NO_PA`) is retained as the default solver recommendation.
